## 📌 What is Input Validation?

**Input validation** is the process of ensuring that **user-provided data** is:
- In the correct **format**
- Of the expected **type**
- Within the allowed **range or constraints**

It's one of the most fundamental and effective ways to prevent common security vulnerabilities like:

- SQL Injection
- Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)
- Command Injection
- Buffer Overflows

---

## 🚨 Why Is It Important?

Unvalidated input is one of the **top causes** of vulnerabilities. Consider this:

(python)
age = input("Enter your age: ")
do_something_with(int(age))

What if the user enters:
"twenty five"

or worse:
"0; rm -rf /"

Without validation, you are blindly trusting the user — and that’s dangerous.

✅ What Does Good Validation Look Like?

Good input validation:
	•	Checks type: string, integer, email, etc.
	•	Enforces length: max 255 characters, minimum 1 character, etc.
	•	Validates format: regex patterns, date formats, allowed characters
	•	Restricts values: whitelist of acceptable values

🔧 Examples of Input Validation

(python)
import re

def is_valid_email(email):
    pattern = r"^[\w\.-]+@[\w\.-]+\.\w+$"
    return re.match(pattern, email) is not None

JavaScript – Numeric Range Validation:
(javascript)
function isValidAge(age) {
  return Number.isInteger(age) && age >= 0 && age <= 120;
}

(C)
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    char input[100];
    int age;
    
    printf("Enter your age: ");
    fgets(input, sizeof(input), stdin);

    // Check if input is a valid integer
    if (sscanf(input, "%d", &age) != 1 || age < 0 || age > 120) {
        printf("Invalid age input!\n");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Valid age: %d\n", age);
    return 0;
}

(C++)
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int age;
    cout << "Enter your age: ";
    
    if (!(cin >> age) || age < 0 || age > 120) {
        cout << "Invalid age input!" << endl;
        return 1;
    }

    cout << "Valid age: " << age << endl;
    return 0;
}

(C#)
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.Write("Enter your age: ");
        string input = Console.ReadLine();
        int age;

        if (!int.TryParse(input, out age) || age < 0 || age > 120)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Invalid age input!");
            return;
        }

        Console.WriteLine($"Valid age: {age}");
    }
}

🌐 Real-World Scenario: Online Registration Form

🧩 Context

You are building a user registration system for a healthcare portal. Each user must provide:
	•	Full name
	•	Email address
	•	Age
	•	National ID (e.g., TC Kimlik No)

Security & reliability are critical — incorrect or malicious input could lead to:
	•	Corrupted records
	•	Security breaches (SQL Injection, XSS)
	•	Invalid user accounts

C# Example: Validating National ID (Must be 11 digits)

Console.Write("Enter your TC Kimlik No: ");
string id = Console.ReadLine();

if (!Regex.IsMatch(id, @"^\d{11}$"))
{
    Console.WriteLine("Invalid TC Kimlik No!");
    return;
}

Console.WriteLine("Valid ID.");
